---
import { LEGAL_EMAIL } from "../../../../lib/legal";

/*
 * The contact form. Panel two of section 2.
 *
 * THE FORM WAS ORIGINALLY DECIDED AGAINST AND THAT DECISION WAS REVERSED. The Website
 * document, section 9, records "no contact form at launch" with reasons — more build,
 * more spam, and a new personal-information collection point the Privacy page would have
 * to describe. Fredrick reversed it on 1 Sept 2026. The third reason did not go away: it
 * was answered, by editing /privacy in the same release. NEVER SHIP THIS PANEL WITHOUT
 * THAT EDIT — a form the Privacy page does not describe makes that page untrue, which is
 * the exact failure the analytics release existed to prevent.
 *
 * NETLIFY FORMS, and the choice is load-bearing rather than convenient. The site is
 * already on Netlify, so this adds no third-party service, no new account, no new row on
 * the Privacy provider table and no server. Google Forms was rejected for two reasons
 * worth keeping: embedding it puts a Google iframe setting cookies on the page BEFORE
 * consent — a second exception to the "nothing loads before Accept" promise, alongside
 * Cal.com — and it cannot be styled to the design system on the one page that exists to
 * establish credibility.
 *
 * HONEYPOT ONLY. NO reCAPTCHA, even though Netlify offers it in one click. reCAPTCHA
 * loads a Google script on page load: a new third party, a new row on /privacy, and a
 * script that would have to sit behind the consent gate — at which point THE FORM BREAKS
 * for anyone who has not accepted cookies. The honeypot costs nothing and creates none of
 * that.
 *
 * THREE FIELDS, AND NO MORE. Every additional field costs submissions. No company field,
 * no phone, no budget, no dropdown, no "how did you hear about us" — if it is worth
 * knowing it is worth asking in the reply. The supplied design shows a fourth field,
 * "Practice Name", and a message label written for accounting firms specifically; neither
 * is built. The design was used for layout, the brief is the copy.
 *
 * REAL <label> ELEMENTS, not placeholder-only fields. A placeholder vanishes the moment
 * someone types, so it cannot carry the question, and it is unreliable for screen
 * readers. Placeholders here are examples only and every one of them is expendable.
 *
 * THE SUBMIT BUTTON IS SECONDARY — transparent with a 1px olive border — and the brief
 * calls that a design-system rule rather than a preference. The page's primary action is
 * the booking button in the panel beside this one. Two primary-fill buttons side by side
 * ask the reader to choose between them, which is the one thing a page with a single
 * primary action must not do. The supplied design fills this button; do not follow it.
 *
 * SUBMITTED BY fetch SO THE PANEL CAN BE REPLACED IN PLACE. A redirect to a thank-you
 * page would mean a page to build, a page to keep out of the sitemap, and a second
 * page_view in the analytics for one enquiry. WITHOUT JAVASCRIPT the form still posts
 * normally and Netlify still receives it — the markup is a real form with a real action,
 * so the enhancement is the in-place state, not the submission itself.
 *
 * THE ERROR STATE IS BUILT, not skipped for being rare. It keeps the form on screen so a
 * retry costs nothing, and it names the email address — an error state that offers no
 * alternative loses the person entirely.
 *
 * SESSION RECORDING: these are the first real input fields on the site. Microsoft Clarity
 * masks inputs in every masking mode and that cannot be switched off, so this should pass
 * — but the standing rule from the analytics release is that masking is VERIFIED in a
 * recording before a form ships, not assumed. That check is Fredrick's and it is on the
 * release list.
 */
---

<div class="form-panel">
  <h2 class="form-panel__heading">Write to us.</h2>

  <p class="form-panel__body">Read by the founder, and answered the same way.</p>

  {/*
    `data-netlify` is what makes Netlify detect this form when it parses the built HTML at
    deploy time. `form-name` as a hidden input is what makes the fetch submission below
    resolve to the same form — Netlify reads it from the body, not from the element.
    `data-netlify-honeypot` names the field a bot will fill and a person never sees.
  */}
  <form
    class="form-panel__form"
    id="contact-form"
    name="contact"
    method="POST"
    data-netlify="true"
    data-netlify-honeypot="bot-field"
  >
    <input type="hidden" name="form-name" value="contact" />

    {/* The honeypot. Hidden from sight and from assistive technology, and never focusable
        by keyboard — a person must not be able to reach it by accident, because filling
        it silently discards their message. */}
    <p class="form-panel__honeypot" aria-hidden="true">
      <label>
        Don&rsquo;t fill this in if you&rsquo;re human
        <input type="text" name="bot-field" tabindex="-1" autocomplete="off" />
      </label>
    </p>

    <div class="form-panel__row">
      <div class="form-panel__field">
        <label class="form-panel__label" for="contact-name">Your name</label>
        <input
          class="form-panel__input"
          id="contact-name"
          name="name"
          type="text"
          autocomplete="name"
          required
        />
      </div>

      <div class="form-panel__field">
        <label class="form-panel__label" for="contact-email">Email address</label>
        <input
          class="form-panel__input"
          id="contact-email"
          name="email"
          type="email"
          autocomplete="email"
          required
        />
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="form-panel__field">
      <label class="form-panel__label" for="contact-message">Your message</label>
      {/* The approved "three lines is enough" copy, reused as helper text. It sits ABOVE
          the field and is wired to it with aria-describedby, so a screen reader hears the
          guidance as part of the field rather than as loose text after it. */}
      <p class="form-panel__help" id="contact-message-help">
        Three lines is enough: what your business does, which process is costing you, and
        what you&rsquo;ve already tried. That&rsquo;s the difference between a reply that
        helps and a reply asking what you meant.
      </p>
      <textarea
        class="form-panel__input form-panel__textarea"
        id="contact-message"
        name="message"
        rows="5"
        aria-describedby="contact-message-help"
        required></textarea>
    </div>

    {/* Hidden until a send fails. `role="alert"` so it is announced the moment it appears
        — a visually placed message above a button is invisible to someone who has just
        pressed that button and cannot see. */}
    <p class="form-panel__error" id="contact-error" role="alert" hidden>
      <span class="form-panel__term">That didn&rsquo;t send.</span> Email{" "}
      <a class="form-panel__email" href={`mailto:${LEGAL_EMAIL}`}>{LEGAL_EMAIL}</a>{" "}
      directly and it will reach us.
    </p>

    <button class="form-panel__submit" type="submit">Send your message</button>

    {/* CASL work, at the point of collection. The longer version in section 3 stays —
        this one exists because a promise three sections below the field it describes is a
        promise the person submitting never read. */}
    <p class="form-panel__consent">
      What you send is used to answer you and nothing else. We don&rsquo;t add you to a
      mailing list. See our <a class="form-panel__link" href="/privacy">Privacy Policy</a>.
    </p>
  </form>

  {/* The success state. Replaces the form in place rather than redirecting. */}
  <div class="form-panel__success" id="contact-success" role="status" hidden>
    <p class="form-panel__success-title">Thank you &mdash; that&rsquo;s arrived.</p>
    <p class="form-panel__body">
      A person will reply, usually within one business day.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>

<script>
  const form = document.getElementById("contact-form") as HTMLFormElement | null;
  const success = document.getElementById("contact-success");
  const error = document.getElementById("contact-error");

  form?.addEventListener("submit", async (event) => {
    event.preventDefault();
    error?.setAttribute("hidden", "");

    const submit = form.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>(".form-panel__submit");
    const original = submit?.textContent ?? "";
    if (submit) {
      submit.disabled = true;
      submit.textContent = "Sending…";
    }

    try {
      /*
       * Netlify accepts the post at any path on the site as long as the body carries
       * `form-name`. Posting to the page's own URL keeps it same-origin, so nothing here
       * is a third-party request and none of it is gated by consent.
       */
      const response = await fetch(form.getAttribute("action") || window.location.pathname, {
        method: "POST",
        headers: { "Content-Type": "application/x-www-form-urlencoded" },
        body: new URLSearchParams(new FormData(form) as unknown as Record<string, string>).toString(),
      });

      if (!response.ok) throw new Error(String(response.status));

      form.setAttribute("hidden", "");
      success?.removeAttribute("hidden");
      /* Move focus to the confirmation. Without this a keyboard or screen-reader user is
       * left focused on a button that no longer exists, with no announcement of what
       * happened. */
      success?.setAttribute("tabindex", "-1");
      (success as HTMLElement | null)?.focus();
    } catch {
      error?.removeAttribute("hidden");
      if (submit) {
        submit.disabled = false;
        submit.textContent = original;
      }
    }
  });
</script>

<style>
  .form-panel {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
  }

  /* Display heading with a rule under it, matching the green half and the design. A
   * treatment change only — the words are the brief's. */
  .form-panel__heading {
    padding-bottom: 16px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--ark-hairline);
    font-family: var(--ark-font-display);
    font-size: var(--ark-h3);
    line-height: var(--ark-lh-h3);
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    color: var(--ark-ink);
  }

  .form-panel__success-title {
    font-family: var(--ark-font-display);
    font-size: var(--ark-h3);
    line-height: var(--ark-lh-h3);
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    color: var(--ark-ink);
  }

  .form-panel__body {
    margin-top: 18px;
    font-size: var(--ark-body);
    line-height: var(--ark-lh-body);
    color: var(--ark-body-ink);
  }

  .form-panel__term {
    color: var(--ark-ink);
    font-weight: 700;
  }

  .form-panel__form {
    margin-top: 24px;
  }

  /* Off-screen rather than display:none. A bot reading the markup still finds and fills
   * it; a person never sees it and cannot tab into it. */
  .form-panel__honeypot {
    position: absolute;
    width: 1px;
    height: 1px;
    overflow: hidden;
    clip-path: inset(50%);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  /* Name and email share a row above 900px, the message runs full width. Two short
   * fields stacked waste the panel's width and push the button below the fold. */
  .form-panel__row {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 16px;
  }

  .form-panel__field + .form-panel__field,
  .form-panel__row + .form-panel__field {
    margin-top: 18px;
  }

  .form-panel__label {
    display: block;
    margin-bottom: 7px;
    font-size: var(--ark-small);
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--ark-ink);
  }

  .form-panel__help {
    margin-bottom: 10px;
    font-size: var(--ark-small);
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--ark-muted);
  }

  /*
   * Filled and borderless, per the design. The fill IS the affordance — a warm panel on a
   * white card reads as somewhere to type without a drawn box around it.
   *
   * The transparent border is not decoration: it holds the box's size so that the focus
   * state can colour it without the field growing by 2px and nudging everything below it.
   * Removing it makes every field jump on focus.
   */
  .form-panel__input {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    padding: 14px 15px;
    font-family: var(--ark-font-body);
    font-size: var(--ark-body);
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--ark-ink);
    background: var(--ark-stage-warm);
    border: 1px solid transparent;
    border-radius: var(--ark-radius-sm);
  }

  .form-panel__input::placeholder {
    color: var(--ark-muted-2);
  }

  .form-panel__textarea {
    resize: vertical;
    min-height: 128px;
  }

  .form-panel__input:focus-visible,
  .form-panel__submit:focus-visible,
  .form-panel__link:focus-visible,
  .form-panel__email:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--ark-olive);
    outline-offset: 2px;
  }

  /*
   * SECONDARY. Transparent fill, 1px olive border — see the note at the top of this file.
   * The primary-fill button on this page is the booking CTA in the panel beside this one,
   * and there is exactly one of those.
   */
  .form-panel__submit {
    margin-top: 22px;
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    padding: 16px 26px;
    font-family: var(--ark-font-body);
    font-size: 1rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    line-height: 1.2;
    color: var(--ark-olive);
    background: transparent;
    border: 1px solid var(--ark-olive);
    border-radius: var(--ark-radius-sm);
    cursor: pointer;
    transition: background-color 0.15s ease, color 0.15s ease;
  }

  .form-panel__submit:hover:not(:disabled) {
    background: var(--ark-olive);
    color: var(--ark-on-dark);
  }

  .form-panel__submit:disabled {
    cursor: default;
    opacity: 0.6;
  }

  .form-panel__error {
    margin-top: 20px;
    padding: 14px 16px;
    font-size: var(--ark-small);
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--ark-body-ink);
    background: var(--ark-gold-bg);
    border: 1px solid var(--ark-gold-border);
    border-radius: var(--ark-radius-sm);
  }

  .form-panel__error[hidden] {
    display: none;
  }

  .form-panel__consent {
    margin-top: 16px;
    font-size: var(--ark-small);
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--ark-muted);
  }

  .form-panel__link,
  .form-panel__email {
    color: var(--ark-olive);
    font-weight: 600;
    text-underline-offset: 3px;
    /* An email address is one unbroken token; without this it can push the card wider
     * than the viewport at 390, and a wide child widens documentElement.scrollWidth. */
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  .form-panel__success[hidden] {
    display: none;
  }

  .form-panel__success:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--ark-olive);
    outline-offset: 4px;
  }

  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .form-panel__row {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
      gap: 18px;
    }

    .form-panel__submit {
      width: 100%;
    }
  }
</style>
